WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.359
A shell has to run ls, with its output going to a file.

2
00:00:06.359 --> 00:00:08.687
How does one program start another?

3
00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:10.994
On Linux it takes three calls.

4
00:00:10.994 --> 00:00:12.250
The first is fork.

5
00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:16.088
It makes a copy of the calling process, with a new PID.

6
00:00:16.267 --> 00:00:18.971
Both copies return from the same fork.

7
00:00:18.971 --> 00:00:21.532
The parent gets the child's PID, 42.

8
00:00:21.532 --> 00:00:25.801
The child gets 0, and that's how each one knows which it is.

9
00:00:25.967 --> 00:00:28.485
Copying all its memory would be slow.

10
00:00:28.485 --> 00:00:34.407
So the kernel copies nothing: both processes point at the same pages, marked read only.

11
00:00:34.567 --> 00:00:39.494
Only when one of them writes to a page does the kernel copy that one page.

12
00:00:39.494 --> 00:00:40.959
This is copy on write.

13
00:00:41.133 --> 00:00:44.162
The child is still running the shell's code.

14
00:00:44.162 --> 00:00:50.427
So it sets itself up first: it opens files.txt, and points descriptor 1, its output, at it.

15
00:00:50.633 --> 00:00:52.533
Then the child calls exec.

16
00:00:52.533 --> 00:00:57.282
The kernel throws away the shell's code and memory, and loads ls.

17
00:00:57.282 --> 00:01:00.424
The PID stays 42, and so do the open files.

18
00:01:00.567 --> 00:01:05.880
ls writes to descriptor 1, as always, and its lines land in the file.

19
00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:06.959
ls never knew.

20
00:01:07.133 --> 00:01:09.781
Meanwhile the parent sleeps in wait.

21
00:01:09.781 --> 00:01:18.752
When ls exits with status 0, the kernel frees its memory and files, but keeps a small record: the PID and the exit status.

22
00:01:18.933 --> 00:01:23.145
wait hands the status to the parent, and the kernel deletes the record.

23
00:01:23.145 --> 00:01:26.883
That's reaping, and it's where dollar question mark gets its 0.

24
00:01:27.033 --> 00:01:30.338
If the parent never waits, that record stays.

25
00:01:30.338 --> 00:01:36.433
The dead child is a zombie: it uses no CPU and can't be killed, but it holds a PID.

26
00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:41.172
Leak one per call, and the container eventually runs out of PIDs.

27
00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:48.916
fork copies cheaply, exec swaps the program, and a dead child waits as a zombie until its parent collects it.
